hing out at the end of their 10  or 12 hour shift and then coming right  back in and working another few hours  and we we had some people who actually  made themselves ill in the process but  everybody knew what the name of the game  was and NASA did a great thing in having  the astronauts visit the plant oh I  think about every second a third week so  it became a question of we are building  this vehicle for someone we know not for  some agency that is a bunch of  bureaucrats somewhere now back to Claude  well the low point in in some days was  going to Houston to make a report on how  we were doing and having to get up there  and say well we've lost another couple  of weeks and it's not easy but you know  that's the way things were and we did  what we had to I  speaking of Houston I remember one such  meeting where one of the fellows who was  involved in a navigation guidance group  was suffering from an earache and I  thought I had arranged it so that he  wouldn't go on that trip but he went  anyhow and he was under medication and  he got up to the podium to talk about  what they were doing and he was so  relaxed from the medication that he  created us sensational impressions  but he knew what he was talking about he  got away with that and afterwards  somebody asked me what is he on but but  that was the kind of spirit that we had  in the group and gives you a couple of  ideas of what went on Earl  well that's a hard question to answer  the rule because you see I never became  an expert at anything but I do remember  very clearly professor Joe Newell who  taught structures and who was a very  conservative man and when he went to  Washington he took the train and that  made a great impression a great  impression yes when you comment it on  some of the things that might have  transitioned on maybe the success and  failures of translating the system into  the well the the question in case you  didn't hear it back there was we were  able to translate some of the sys